# 12 — UI Styles

> **For the agent reading this:** Read `plans/11-distribution` first (the Control Panel, `src/control-panel/`, and the packaged app), then look at `src/control-panel/styles.css` and the hero in `src/control-panel/App.tsx`: that's the look this phase brings to the editor. The editor (`src/web/styles.css`, about 1300 lines) is today a dark, translucent, system-font UI over a warm off-white 3D view, with its colors written out by hand (5 variables, 40-odd more hex and rgba values in the TSX files). This phase adds:
> - **one theme for both apps:** the Control Panel's tokens (paper and ink, the six block pastels with their dark shades, Fredoka / Nunito / JetBrains Mono) in a shared module both apps import, with the fonts bundled (the Control Panel loads them from Google Fonts today, which a downloaded, local-first app shouldn't);
> - **the editor in the block style:** chunky frames (ink edges, hard block shadows, pressable block buttons) around calm, compact rows;
> - **light and dark:** the editor follows the system, like the Control Panel, with a toggle to override it;
> - **the wordmark** (**orla BLOCKS**): small at the editor's top left, large and animated on the project picker and the welcome screen;
> - **the app icon** (`src/assets/orla_blocks_icon.png`, Orlando's): the Tauri app's icons and both apps' favicons.
>
> The 3D view is **not** restyled: the canvas background, the grid, the shape palette and what `render_view` and shots capture stay exactly as they are (the agent's eyes and the palette's keys are a contract). Only what the editor draws over the level (the selection, drafts, the Pen, point and array handles, the marquee) moves to the theme's accent colors. The work is split into small increments (§9). Each one ends with the editor fully working and a browser checklist for Orlando. Add this phase's terms to `GLOSSARY.md` (marked "from 12") as they land. Log progress in §11 at the end of this file.

